  "description": {
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (en): Astragalus brachycalyx",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astragalus_brachycalyx",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 1370224799,
      "text": "Astragalus brachycalyx, the Persian manna or manna, whose name is derived from the Greek ‘brachy’ meaning \"short\" and ‘calyx’ meaning the sepal of a flower, is a species of legume commonly found on rocky mountain slopes in western Asia, from western Iran and northern Iraq to Turkey, and is commonly used as a source of gum tragacanth. The sweet honeydew of the plant lice Cyamophila astragalicola secreted onto A. brachycalyx is an essential component of traditional gaz, the Persian form of nougat produced in Isfahan, Iran."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Astragalus brachycalyx",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astragalus_brachycalyx",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 117084885,
      "text": "Astragalus brachycalyx es una especie de legumbre que comúnmente se encuentra en las laderas rocosas de las montañas en el oeste de Asia, desde el oeste de Irán y el noreste de Irak hasta Turquía. Se la utiliza con frecuencia para preparar goma tragacanto."
    }
  },
